### Dual-LCD Master Firmware

Once you have bridged pad `A0` on your second display (changing its address to `0x26`), you can instantiate two independent `LiquidCrystal_I2C` objects in your Arduino sketch:

```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <LiquidCrystal_I2C.h>

// Primary Display: Factory Default Address
LiquidCrystal_I2C lcdPrimary(0x27, 16, 2);

// Secondary Display: Jumper A0 Bridged to Ground
LiquidCrystal_I2C lcdSecondary(0x26, 16, 2);

int missionSeconds = 0;
float robotBattery = 12.6;

void setup() {
  Wire.begin();

  // Initialize Primary Display (0x27)
  lcdPrimary.init();
  lcdPrimary.backlight();
  lcdPrimary.setCursor(0, 0);
  lcdPrimary.print("MISSION HUD #1");
  lcdPrimary.setCursor(0, 1);
  lcdPrimary.print("ADDR: 0x27 OK");

  // Initialize Secondary Display (0x26)
  lcdSecondary.init();
  lcdSecondary.backlight();
  lcdSecondary.setCursor(0, 0);
  lcdSecondary.print("TELEMETRY HUD #2");
  lcdSecondary.setCursor(0, 1);
  lcdSecondary.print("ADDR: 0x26 OK");

  delay(2000);
  lcdPrimary.clear();
  lcdSecondary.clear();
}

void loop() {
  missionSeconds++;
  robotBattery -= 0.01; // Simulate battery drain

  // Update Screen 1: Mission Clock
  lcdPrimary.setCursor(0, 0);
  lcdPrimary.print("MISSION CLOCK:");
  lcdPrimary.setCursor(0, 1);
  lcdPrimary.print("ELAPSED: ");
  lcdPrimary.print(missionSeconds);
  lcdPrimary.print("s   ");

  // Update Screen 2: Robot Power Telemetry
  lcdSecondary.setCursor(0, 0);
  lcdSecondary.print("POWER SYSTEM:");
  lcdSecondary.setCursor(0, 1);
  lcdSecondary.print("BATT: ");
  lcdSecondary.print(robotBattery, 2);
  lcdSecondary.print("V  ");

  delay(1000);
}
```

### Code Deconstruction:
- `lcdPrimary(0x27, 16, 2)` & `lcdSecondary(0x26, 16, 2)`: Creates two distinct C++ driver instances targeting different physical devices on the exact same two wires.
- Zero address collisions occur because each transaction targets its own verified 7-bit address.

